---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="About | The Rang Yan Review"
  description="About The Rang Yan Review — our mission, principles, and commitment to independent literary culture."
>
  <Header />

  <main id="main-content">
    <div class="container about-page">
      <header class="about-header">
        <p class="category-label">The Journal</p>
        <h1 class="about-title">About <em>The Rang Yan Review</em></h1>
      </header>

      <div class="about-body">
        <section class="about-section">
          <p>
            <em>Rang Yan Review</em> is an independent literary and philosophical journal devoted to critical thought, humanist culture, and the life of language.
          </p>
          <p>
            We publish poetry, fiction, essays, criticism, philosophy, and experimental writing. What brings these different forms together is a shared curiosity about the relationship between aesthetic form and the material conditions in which culture comes into being: who has the time to write, who gets to publish, who gets read, who is excluded, and what happens to a work once it enters the world.
          </p>
          <p>
            We have never been very comfortable with the idea of literature as a protected space somewhere outside history. A poem is written by someone, somewhere, at a particular moment. It carries a language, a memory, a social world. It can reproduce the assumptions of that world, or it can make those assumptions suddenly visible. Sometimes a work does this deliberately; sometimes it happens almost against the writer's intentions. We are interested in that tension.
          </p>
          <p>
            Form matters to us because politics is already present in language. It appears in what can be said and what remains difficult to say, in whose experiences acquire a vocabulary and whose remain unnamed, in the rhythms and structures through which a life becomes recognizable. Literature can preserve what power would prefer to forget. It can also make familiar forms of power strange again.
          </p>
          <p>
            We believe literature and philosophy have a real place in collective life. They give people ways to remember, to think together, to imagine lives different from the ones available to them, and sometimes simply to find words for something they have felt for years without knowing how to name it. We would like to protect that space from the constant demand that everything become useful, measurable, marketable, or immediately understandable.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is also why we are interested in the material life of culture. Books cost money to produce. Writers need time. Editors work. Translators work. Publishers, libraries, universities, festivals, journals, and cultural institutions all shape what becomes visible. Some people are given enormous amounts of cultural space; others have to fight to enter it. Literature does not float above these conditions.
          </p>
          <p>
            We are not a party organ, and we have no party line. We do not ask writers to demonstrate their political correctness, nor do we expect every text we publish to arrive with a recognizable political message. At the same time, we do not believe that culture exists in a neutral space. The literary field is shaped by property, class, labour, institutions, access, exclusion, canonisation, and the unequal distribution of attention. Even deciding what deserves to be remembered is already a social act.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our sympathies lie with Marxist and anti-capitalist thought, internationalist traditions of the left, anti-authoritarian communism, libertarian socialism, feminist and anti-colonial struggles, and other emancipatory traditions concerned with overcoming relations of domination. We are interested in politics that asks how the conditions producing inequality might actually be changed, rather than simply making those inequalities somewhat more comfortable.
          </p>
          <p>
            We also know that the traditions we value are full of arguments, disagreements, failures, blind spots, and unresolved questions. We prefer them that way. Marxism, feminism, anti-colonial thought, ecological politics, queer theory, anarchism, and other emancipatory traditions are not monuments to be approached with reverence. They are bodies of thought that continue to argue with themselves and with one another. We read them in that spirit — taking what they can teach us, questioning what no longer holds, and allowing different traditions to meet without forcing them into a single vocabulary.
          </p>
          <p>
            We are especially drawn to writing that changes something about the way reality can be perceived. A text can speak about poverty, migration, gender, violence, labour, ecology, or exclusion and still leave the reader's habits of perception completely untouched. We are looking for something more difficult: writing in which the form itself begins to shift under the pressure of what it is trying to encounter.
          </p>
          <p>
            Sometimes this means an unfamiliar syntax. Sometimes it means a voice that refuses the categories available to it. Sometimes it means a story that cannot be reduced to a lesson. Sometimes it is simply a sentence that makes the familiar world look strange for a moment.
          </p>
          <p>
            We want writing that takes people seriously as complicated, unfinished beings. People whose lives exceed their economic roles. People who cannot be adequately described as consumers, workers, citizens, victims, representatives, or members of a demographic category. We are interested in the places where language begins to fail these classifications — and in what might become possible there.
          </p>
          <p>
            Ultimately, <em>Rang Yan Review</em> is a space for attention. We want to read slowly enough to notice what a text is doing, and to make room for writing that has not yet learned how it is supposed to behave. We are interested in difficulty, beauty, contradiction, experiment, memory, and the strange life that a piece of writing can acquire once it leaves the person who wrote it.
          </p>
          <p>
            The journal is political because literature already lives in a political world. It is humanist because we remain interested in the irreducible strangeness of individual lives. And it is literary because we believe that sometimes the form of a sentence can tell us something about the world that a political statement cannot.
          </p>

          <h2 class="about-heading">The Past and Future</h2>
          <p>
            We publish contemporary writing alongside work from earlier historical periods. We have always felt that the past is less settled than it appears.
          </p>
          <p>
            Literature lives in the movement between times. Forms, ideas, memories, conflicts, and unfinished hopes pass from one generation to another. They change as they travel. We change too, and that changes the texts we read.
          </p>
          <p>
            We return to canonical writers, of course, but also to forgotten journals, working-class voices, feminist interventions, anti-colonial texts, dissident writers, defeated movements, experimental forms, small presses, marginal publications, and books that disappeared almost as soon as they were printed. The archive has its own politics. What survives is never simply what was most valuable. Preservation depends on institutions, money, access, prestige, language, and chance.
          </p>
          <p>
            Sometimes the forgotten material is where the most interesting possibilities are hiding.
          </p>
          <p>
            A lost or neglected text can remind us that the present was never inevitable. People in the past made choices without knowing where those choices would lead. They imagined other arrangements of work, family, education, sexuality, community, and political life. Some of those experiments failed. Some were suppressed. Some simply disappeared.
          </p>
          <p>
            Their disappearance does not make them meaningless.
          </p>
          <p>
            Reading them now can widen our sense of what a society has been capable of imagining.
          </p>
          <p>
            The present is doing something similar in front of us. New forms of labour and precarity, migration and displacement, technological change, ecological crisis, changing forms of gender and collective life — these experiences are often moving faster than the language available to describe them. Writers feel this pressure early. Sometimes a new experience first appears as a disturbance in form: a broken syntax, an unfamiliar voice, a strange rhythm, a story that refuses to move in the way we expect.
          </p>
          <p>
            We are interested in those disturbances.
          </p>
          <p>
            They may tell us something about where the present is going.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is where the future enters our understanding of <em>Rang Yan</em>. We do not think of the future as a finished picture waiting somewhere ahead of us. It is closer to a field of possibilities, many of which are still difficult to see. Some are already present in small and fragile forms: a different way of organising work, a new kind of solidarity, an unfamiliar relationship between people and technology, a language for a desire that has not yet found a public name.
          </p>
          <p>
            The future often begins before we recognise it as the future.
          </p>
          <p>
            That is why we are wary of political visions that describe tomorrow with too much certainty. There is something seductive about a perfectly imagined society. It gives the present a clear direction and makes uncertainty disappear. But real lives have a way of exceeding plans. People improvise. Circumstances change. Unexpected solidarities appear. New needs become visible. A possibility that seemed marginal becomes necessary.
          </p>
          <p>
            <em>Rang Yan</em> gives us a language for remaining attentive to this process. To let something unfold is to give it enough space to become more than our first idea of it. It asks for patience, but also for attention. We stay close to what is happening without pretending that we already know what it will become.
          </p>
          <p>
            This has a political meaning for us. Capitalism is extraordinarily skilled at imagining the future in terms of markets, investment, consumption, efficiency, and technological expansion. The future arrives already packaged as a product. Institutions often approach it through prediction, management, and risk. We are told what tomorrow will require of us long before tomorrow has actually arrived.
          </p>
          <p>
            We want to preserve another possibility.
          </p>
          <p>
            A left politics needs imagination because the world we live in is not the only world that could exist. Housing, work, healthcare, education, care, migration, ecology, time — all of these can be organised differently. The point is not to draw the perfect society on paper. The point is to make more forms of collective life materially possible.
          </p>
          <p>
            For us, emancipation begins there: in expanding the range of lives people can actually live.
          </p>
          <p>
            Literature has a particular role in this process because it can hold open possibilities before they become political programs. A novel can allow us to inhabit a life that does not yet have social legitimacy. A poem can make a feeling visible before we have a name for it. An essay can connect experiences that seemed unrelated. Sometimes a literary work changes the horizon of what feels imaginable without ever telling us what we should do.
          </p>
          <p>
            The past, present, and future meet in these moments.
          </p>
          <p>
            The past contains futures that never happened. The present contains possibilities we have not yet learned to recognise. And our image of the future changes the questions we ask of the past. History becomes a little less like a museum and a little more like a conversation across time.
          </p>
          <p>
            Perhaps this is what we mean by <em>Rang Yan</em>: staying with what is unfinished long enough for it to surprise us. Allowing an old text to speak in a new situation. Giving contemporary writing enough freedom to invent forms we cannot yet classify. Making room for futures that have not learned how to announce themselves.
          </p>
          <p>
            We do not know what will emerge from that openness. We would rather keep that uncertainty alive than fill it too quickly.
          </p>
          <p>
            A journal, after all, is a small thing. It cannot determine the future. It cannot settle the meaning of the past. It can read, publish, translate, remember, connect people, and create a little space in which something unexpected might happen.
          </p>
          <p>
            For us, that space is already worthwhile.
          </p>
          <p>
            <em>Rang Yan Review</em> wants to remain there: somewhere between memory and possibility, between what has been inherited and what might still emerge.
          </p>

          <h2 class="about-heading"><em>Rang Yan</em></h2>
          <p>
            The name <em>Rang Yan</em> comes from the Tibetan expression <em>rang yan</em> (རང་ཡན), which carries meanings such as “to let be, to abandon, to treat casually,” “naturally expansive, naturally unfolding, naturally dissipating,” and “free and relaxed.”
          </p>
          <p>
            We were drawn to the phrase because it suggests a different way of being with things. There is something generous in it, but also something difficult. To let something be means allowing it to remain partly outside our control. It means giving up, at least for a moment, the satisfaction of knowing exactly what something is, where it is going, and what it is supposed to become.
          </p>
          <p>
            This matters to us in language, culture, and politics.
          </p>
          <p>
            The left has a long relationship with the idea of mastery. We have wanted to understand history, to organize society, to give collective life a direction. There is something admirable in that desire: the refusal to accept exploitation and humiliation as inevitable facts of life. Yet the same desire can become dangerous when the future is imagined too clearly, when a political project begins to speak in the name of people who have not yet spoken for themselves.
          </p>
          <p>
            The history of the twentieth century makes this difficult to ignore. Again and again, emancipatory hopes became entangled with institutions of discipline, with political languages that demanded certainty, with the belief that a better society could be designed in advance. The state and capital have their own versions of this certainty. They classify, measure, regulate, predict. They make the world legible because legibility makes it governable.
          </p>
          <p>
            We do not think the answer is to withdraw from collective life. We need solidarity, organization, institutions, and material change. People need housing, healthcare, education, time, dignity, and the freedom to shape their own lives. The question is what kind of politics can pursue these things while leaving room for what cannot yet be anticipated.
          </p>
          <p>
            We are interested in that space.
          </p>
          <p>
            A materialist politics begins with attention. It looks at what is already taking shape beneath the dominant order: forms of life that have not yet found a language, desires that have been disciplined out of existence, forms of solidarity that may look insignificant until someone learns how to see them. History is full of things that appeared impossible until they became ordinary.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is where <em>rang yan</em> becomes useful to us. It suggests patience, but a particular kind of patience. We wait while remaining attentive. We allow something to unfold without assuming that we already know its destination.
          </p>
          <p>
            That distinction matters. Leaving existing relations untouched can simply mean accepting the world as it is. <em>Rang yan</em> asks something more difficult: how can we intervene in the conditions of life while resisting the temptation to determine every possible outcome?
          </p>
          <p>
            Nothing unfolds in a vacuum. Every life is shaped by material conditions, by bodies and institutions, by history, money, borders, language, memory, and power. Freedom has never existed outside these things. What matters is the possibility of changing the conditions themselves, so that lives can acquire forms that were previously unavailable to them.
          </p>
          <p>
            We also inherit a world before we have the chance to choose it. We inherit languages we did not invent, histories we did not make, wounds we did not cause, and forms of beauty that were already waiting for us. Some of what we inherit sustains us. Some of it hurts. Often the two are inseparable.
          </p>
          <p class="about-epigraph">
            We inherit the most luminous layers —<br />
            and we spend our lives trying to accept the rest.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is one of the difficulties of cultural inheritance. We cannot simply preserve everything, and we cannot simply discard everything. A living culture is made through this uncertain movement between attachment and criticism, memory and change. Sometimes we keep something because it is beautiful. Sometimes we keep it because we have not yet found the words to let it go. Sometimes we discover that what we thought belonged to the past is still alive inside us.
          </p>
          <p>
            Perhaps letting be has something to do with this as well: allowing an inheritance to remain complicated.
          </p>
          <p>
            Capitalism has very little patience for such complications. It wants to know what something can do, what it can produce, how quickly it can circulate, what it is worth. It turns time into schedules and attention into a resource. It teaches us to experience waiting as failure and slowness as inefficiency.
          </p>
          <p>
            There are moments when doing nothing is treated as a moral defect.
          </p>
          <p>
            We find this pressure everywhere: in work, in education, in cultural production, and increasingly in the way people are expected to understand themselves. We are asked to optimize our bodies, our careers, our relationships, even our thoughts. The question “What are you doing with your life?” often carries another question underneath it: “What are you producing from it?”
          </p>
          <p>
            There should be room for another answer.
          </p>
          <p>
            There should be room for uselessness, hesitation, opacity, wandering, and unfinished thought.
          </p>
          <p>
            The state has its own appetite for legibility. It wants names, categories, documents, identities, statistics. Institutions need information in order to function, but information can also become a form of power. A person becomes easier to manage when they can be reduced to a category.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is why we are interested in opacity. Some things deserve to remain partially unknown. A person should be allowed to contain more than the description assigned to them. A desire does not always need to become an identity. A community does not have to make itself completely transparent in order to deserve recognition.
          </p>
          <p>
            There is freedom in the parts of ourselves that remain untranslated.
          </p>
          <p>
            This also changes how we think about literature.
          </p>
          <p>
            We have no particular interest in turning literature into an instrument of political correctness, political education, or political persuasion. Literature can certainly enter political life. It can disturb a language, expose an assumption, preserve a memory, give shape to an experience that had previously seemed impossible to articulate. But its value does not depend on its ability to deliver a message.
          </p>
          <p>
            Sometimes a poem is important because it refuses to tell us what to think.
          </p>
          <p>
            Sometimes a novel gives us something we cannot immediately use.
          </p>
          <p>
            Sometimes a sentence stays with us for years without becoming an argument.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is one reason we want <em>Rang Yan Review</em> to remain open to difficult, contradictory, unfinished writing. We want texts that take risks with language, texts that do not always explain themselves, texts whose meanings may change depending on who reads them and when.
          </p>
          <p>
            We also want criticism to retain some of that uncertainty. A critic can illuminate a work without closing it down. Reading can be a form of companionship. You stay with something long enough for it to change your understanding of what you thought you already knew.
          </p>
          <p>
            And perhaps this is where literature begins to complicate the idea of ownership.
          </p>
          <p>
            A writer makes a work, but cannot determine its entire life. Once a poem enters another person's memory, it has already travelled somewhere beyond its author. A translation changes it. A misreading changes it. Time changes it. A stranger may find in it something the writer never intended.
          </p>
          <p>
            Literature has always moved like this: across borders, languages, generations, classes, bodies. It is inherited, translated, borrowed, transformed, misunderstood, and given away.
          </p>
          <p>
            Capital would prefer to turn all of this movement into property. Institutions may try to attach literature to nations, traditions, identities, markets, or reputations. Yet something in literature keeps escaping these arrangements.
          </p>
          <p>
            A book can be printed by a corporation and still become someone's private refuge. A poem written in one century can suddenly become necessary to someone living in another. A language that seemed marginal can become the language in which a stranger finally recognizes themselves.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is why we think literature matters politically. It creates forms of relation that cannot be completely predicted or administered.
          </p>
          <p>
            And perhaps this is also what we mean by solidarity. We do not have to become identical in order to stand beside one another. We do not need to agree about everything before we can recognize another person's vulnerability, imagination, or right to exist. A collective can contain disagreement. It can leave room for people to remain strange to one another.
          </p>
          <p>
            We are aware of the irony here. A journal devoted to “letting be” is itself an institution. We select texts. We edit them. We publish them under a name. We inevitably make choices about what enters this space and what does not.
          </p>
          <p>
            We cannot escape that contradiction, and we do not want to pretend otherwise.
          </p>
          <p>
            Perhaps <em>Rang Yan</em> is valuable precisely because it gives us a way to remain conscious of the contradiction. We will inevitably shape the space we create. The question is whether that shaping leaves room for surprise.
          </p>
          <p>
            We want this journal to be such a space: shaped, but not sealed; political, but not doctrinaire; attentive to history without believing that history has already told us what comes next.
          </p>
          <p>
            Perhaps the left begins somewhere around here. It begins with the willingness to listen before speaking for others. It begins with curiosity about forms of life that do not yet have a recognized political vocabulary. It begins with the possibility that the people who will transform our understanding of freedom may not yet know that they are doing so.
          </p>
          <p>
            We do not know in advance what freedom will look like.
          </p>
          <p>
            That uncertainty can be frightening. It can also be a source of hope.
          </p>
          <p>
            We are interested in what might emerge when there is enough space for something unexpected to become possible.
          </p>
          <p>
            And so we come back to literature.
          </p>
          <p>
            A poem cannot belong entirely to its author once it has entered the world. A language cannot be permanently owned by the people who first spoke it. A story can cross a border without asking permission. A sentence can survive the disappearance of the person who wrote it and find a home in someone they never knew.
          </p>
          <p class="about-epigraph">
            Literature does not belong to anyone.<br />
            Literature belongs to everyone.
          </p>
          <p>
            Perhaps that is the small paradox at the heart of <em>Rang Yan Review</em>: what cannot be possessed can still be shared. What remains singular can become collective. What begins as one person's voice can become part of another person's life.
          </p>
          <p>
            We do not want to close this possibility with a final definition.
          </p>
          <p>
            <em>Rang Yan</em> is not an answer.
          </p>
          <p>
            It is a way of keeping the question open.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>

<style>
  .about-page {
    padding: 4rem 0 0;
  }

  .about-header {
    margin-bottom: 3.5rem;
    padding-bottom: 2rem;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--color-ink);
    max-width: 60ch;
  }

  .about-header .category-label {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .about-title {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 2.75rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    margin: 0;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .about-title em {
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .about-body {
    max-width: 68ch;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0;
  }

  .about-section {
    padding-bottom: 0;
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .about-section p {
    font-family: var(--font-serif-body);
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.85;
    color: var(--color-ink-secondary);
    margin-bottom: 1em;
  }

  .about-section p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .about-section em {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .about-heading {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem;
    padding-top: 0.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .about-heading em {
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: inherit;
  }

  .about-section .about-epigraph {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    margin: 1.75em 0;
    padding: 0.25em 0 0.25em 1rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--color-burgundy);
  }

  .about-section .about-plain-callout {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-style: normal;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1.65;
    letter-spacing: -0.005em;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    margin: 1.75em 0;
    padding: 0.25em 0 0.25em 1rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--color-burgundy);
  }

  .about-section .about-closing {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    margin-top: 1.75em;
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
</style>
